From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Domenico D'Arrigo <d.darrigo@outlook.it>
Date: Fri, 6 Nov 2026 21:30:00 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] security/lockdown: Write-protect SI_ALL with the controller
 as an option

An image built with UNLOCK_FLASH_REGIONS has a descriptor that lets the
host write every region, so that flashrom can rewrite the read-only
part, the ME or the descriptor itself from the OS. The price is that
the OS can also write them at any time: a root process can change the
straps and the permissions of the descriptor, replace or re-enable the
Management Engine, or brick the machine, while the boot media lock
covers WP_RO only and the BIOS lock covers the BIOS region only.

Add BOOTMEDIA_LOCK_SI_ALL: with the controller lock on WP_RO (or the
Top Swap regions), set a second protected range over the SI_ALL FMAP
region, the descriptor with the GbE and the ME regions, write-only, so
they stay readable. The firmware writes them before the lock when an
option asks (the HAP bit, for instance), the ME and the GbE use their
own masters and are not affected, the RW regions stay out of it. With
BOOTMEDIA_LOCK_RUNTIME_OPTION the option that lifts the lock of WP_RO
lifts this one too, for a flashrom update from the OS.

The descriptor's own permissions do not change: chipsec's spi_desc,
which reads them (FRAP.BRWA), still reports them; the protected range
is what refuses the write.

TEST=ThinkPad W541 and ThinkCentre M920x: to be run; expected a second
"FPR n is enabled for range 0x00000000-0x004fffff" (W541) in the log
and flashrom refusing writes to the descriptor, the GbE and the ME
regions while Firmware protection is on.
---
 src/security/lockdown/Kconfig    | 16 ++++++++++++++++
 src/security/lockdown/lockdown.c | 14 ++++++++++++++
 2 files changed, 30 insertions(+)

diff --git a/src/security/lockdown/Kconfig b/src/security/lockdown/Kconfig
index 49ac27415e..cdaf647ca8 100644
--- a/src/security/lockdown/Kconfig
+++ b/src/security/lockdown/Kconfig
@@ -96,6 +96,22 @@ config BOOTMEDIA_LOCK_IN_VERSTAGE
 	  ramstage, like the MRC cache for example.
 	  Use this option if you don't trust code running after verstage.
 
+config BOOTMEDIA_LOCK_SI_ALL
+	bool "Also write-protect the flash descriptor, the GbE and the ME regions"
+	depends on BOOTMEDIA_LOCK_CONTROLLER
+	depends on BOOTMEDIA_LOCK_WPRO_VBOOT_RO || BOOTMEDIA_LOCK_TOPSWAP
+	depends on HAVE_IFD_BIN
+	help
+	  Select this to set a protected range of the controller over the
+	  SI_ALL FMAP region, the flash descriptor with the GbE and the ME
+	  regions, whenever the boot media lock is applied. On an image whose
+	  descriptor leaves those regions writable to the host (for instance
+	  with UNLOCK_FLASH_REGIONS, so that flashrom can write them from the
+	  OS), this keeps the OS from writing them until the next reset; they
+	  stay readable, and the firmware itself can still write them before
+	  the lock is applied. With BOOTMEDIA_LOCK_RUNTIME_OPTION the same
+	  option that lifts the lock of the read-only part lifts this one.
+
 config BOOTMEDIA_LOCK_RUNTIME_OPTION
 	bool "Read whether to lock the boot media from an option"
 	depends on !BOOTMEDIA_LOCK_NONE
diff --git a/src/security/lockdown/lockdown.c b/src/security/lockdown/lockdown.c
index e672dfde44..25e93cbfeb 100644
--- a/src/security/lockdown/lockdown.c
+++ b/src/security/lockdown/lockdown.c
@@ -69,6 +69,20 @@ void boot_device_security_lockdown(void)
 	else
 		printk(BIOS_ERR, "BM-LOCKDOWN: Failed to enable bootmedia protection\n");
 
+	if (CONFIG(BOOTMEDIA_LOCK_SI_ALL)) {
+		/*
+		 * The descriptor, the GbE and the ME regions: when the descriptor
+		 * lets the host write them, only a protected range of the
+		 * controller keeps the OS from doing so. They stay readable.
+		 */
+		if (fmap_locate_area_as_rdev("SI_ALL", &dev) < 0)
+			printk(BIOS_ERR, "BM-LOCKDOWN: Could not find region 'SI_ALL'\n");
+		else if (boot_device_wp_region(&dev, lock_type) >= 0)
+			printk(BIOS_INFO, "BM-LOCKDOWN: Enabled bootmedia protection for SI_ALL\n");
+		else
+			printk(BIOS_ERR, "BM-LOCKDOWN: Failed to enable bootmedia protection for SI_ALL\n");
+	}
+
 	if (CONFIG(BOOTMEDIA_LOCK_TOPSWAP)) {
 		/*
 		 * Additionally set a protected range for the BOOTBLOCK region
-- 
2.43.0

